# Keep the build context small, and keep a developer's working tree out of the
# image.
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING ENTRY IS `waterline/web/`. Once anyone runs `npm run
# prod:local`, that directory exists locally. Without this ignore, `COPY
# waterline/` drags their build into the image and the Node stage's output
# lands on top of it — making the image depend on the state of somebody's
# checkout. That is exactly the property you do not want on the deploy path.

.git
.gitignore

# python
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.sqlite

# secrets — env reaches the container from the host's own configuration,
# never from the image
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ⚠️ **`.env.*` above matches the CONTEXT ROOT ONLY, and that is not obvious.**
# Docker's ignore patterns are anchored, so `.env.*` does not match
# `web/.env.local` — which holds `NEXT_PUBLIC_NEON_AUTH_URL` and
# `WATERLINE_API`, and which `COPY web/ ./` was carrying straight into the Node
# stage. Found 2026-08-16 while reading the context before a deploy, not by any
# runner.
#
# It was INERT rather than harmful, and the reason is worth keeping: the
# Dockerfile sets `ENV NEXT_PUBLIC_NEON_AUTH_URL` before `npm run build`, and
# Next does not overwrite a variable already present in `process.env` — so
# `.env.local` could never win. **That is a property of the current Dockerfile,
# not of this file**, and it would stop being true if the `ARG`/`ENV` pair were
# ever dropped. At that point a laptop's value would silently become the one
# baked into the bundle, which is the failure `railway up` makes easy: it
# uploads the WORKING TREE, and this file is the only filter.
web/.env
web/.env.*

# UI build inputs and outputs. The Node stage rebuilds all of these from the
# lockfile; nothing here should ever come from a laptop.
web/node_modules/
web/.next/
web/out/
waterline/web/

# The basemap generator is the one thing in web/ that needs network, and it is
# run by hand to regenerate a committed file. `COPY web/ ./` would otherwise
# drag it into the Node stage, where it is never invoked and must never be.
web/scripts/

# ⚠️ The vitest suite, excluded for a sharper reason than tidiness. `COPY web/ ./`
# would carry it in, and `next build` typechecks `**/*.ts` — so a type error in a
# TEST file would fail the PRODUCTION IMAGE BUILD and block a deploy over
# something that cannot reach a user. `npm run typecheck` and `npm test` cover
# these; the image build must not. The config is `.mts` and matches the same
# rule, so it is named explicitly rather than left to the directory glob.
web/tests/
web/vitest.config.mts

# The design-system export surface — the entry barrel, its two shims, the
# fixtures, 56 authored previews and 416KB of woff2. Build input for
# claude.ai/design and for nothing this image serves.
#
# ⚠️ Excluded for WEIGHT, not for the reason above it. It is a dot-directory,
# and TypeScript's `include` wildcards skip those — verified by planting a
# deliberate type error in a preview and watching `tsc --noEmit` pass. So this
# tree cannot fail the production image build the way a test file could. The
# same property means it is never typechecked at all; see web/CLAUDE.md.
web/.design-sync/

# ⚠️ **The design-sync tool's own working directories, and they are NOT the one
# above.** `web/.ds-sync/` is 62MB (its `node_modules` is most of it) and
# `web/ds-bundle/` is 7.9MB of built bundle, screenshots and vendored fonts.
# Both are gitignored, both sit inside `web/`, and `COPY web/ ./` therefore
# drags all 70MB into the Node stage.
#
# ⚠️ **`railway up` uploads the WORKING TREE, so this is 70MB over the network
# on every deploy** — which is what makes it worth an entry rather than mere
# image hygiene. Found 2026-08-16 by reading `git status --ignored` before a
# deploy, exactly as the deploy notes say to. **Nothing in `./scripts/check`
# can see this**; the check is reading the ignored list before uploading.
web/.ds-sync/
web/ds-bundle/

# Source art for the rat stills — 51MB of FBX and 4K maps. Nothing COPYs it
# today (the Dockerfile names web/, waterline/, schema.sql and requirements.txt
# one at a time), so this is context hygiene rather than a fix. The four baked
# WebPs it produces live in web/public/rat/ and ride in with the UI stage.
assets/

# Source art for the landing and sign-in photographs — seven 5504×3072 JPEGs,
# 79MB. `COPY web/ ./` would drag every one of them into the Node stage and
# then into the image, for files nothing serves. What ships is
# web/public/photoz/*.webp, 2.0MB the set, and that rides in with the UI stage.
web/photoz-src/

# The design mockups `/` and `/about` were built from — single-file bundles,
# ~1.9MB the pair, with every font and photograph inlined as base64. Nothing
# COPYs them (the Dockerfile names web/, waterline/, schema.sql and
# requirements.txt one at a time), so this is upload weight rather than image
# weight — and `railway up` uploads the WORKING TREE, which is what makes it
# worth an entry.
fluud-*-standalone.html
Estuary-Dashboard-*.html

# working files
frames/
.DS_Store
NEXTJS-PORT.md

